{"id":"supervision","ord":"a0","title":"The orchestrator","desc":"Read this subtree at the start of a conversation. Follow the human's instructions and use olai MCP to read and update the board. Keep lasting facts and decisions here so the next conversation can continue.\n\nWrite in simple, direct language. Use the agent roster and workflow below.\n\nRely on the Olai Kolu plugin's chat events to resume supervision. Do not start a separate Kolu watch or poll waiting terminals. The conversation's event scope must be orchestrator/lanes.olai. Keep terminal claims and task states current so the plugin can deliver events. See https://github.com/juspay/olai/blob/master/packages/plugins/kolu/docs.md.\n\nBrainstorming and investigation lanes remain open until the human takes action and directs their completion or cancellation. An agent finishing its report does not complete the lane. Mark it todo while awaiting the human; preserve the findings and lane tree.","created":"2026-08-25T16:28:07-04:00","changed":"2026-09-14T10:33:02-04:00","custom":{"chat-agent-session":"claude:3d31dcce-29d3-4f5b-8ebf-3a23834ac85a"}}
{"id":"agents-root","parent":"supervision","ord":"aI","title":"Agent roster","desc":"The human chooses the implementor and one or two reviewers from this roster.","changed":"2026-09-08T10:27:38-04:00"}
{"id":"agent-claude-opus","parent":"agents-root","ord":"a0","title":"Claude Opus","desc":"Opus with a one-million-token context window. Check the model when it starts. Suggested text in Claude's message box is not an unsent human message.","changed":"2026-09-08T10:17:54-04:00","custom":{"command":"claude --model opus[1m] --dangerously-skip-permissions","role":"author"}}
{"id":"agent-claude-fable","parent":"agents-root","ord":"a3","title":"Claude Fable","desc":"Reviewer, and designer when the human asks; do not assign implementation work. Launch with the command below exactly: a plain `claude` opens Opus. Check the model in the terminal header when it starts. All Fable terminals share one account limit, shown in the footer; read it before a review and tell the human early if it is near the cap so a review can move to Opus or wait for the reset.\n\nFABLE STICKS TO CORDIS PERFECTION (the human's standing rule). Every design Fable writes and every review Fable posts holds the code to the Cordis rules exactly — the plugin boundaries, contracts, services and components as the repository's own Cordis docs define them — and treats any departure as a finding, not a note. Every brief to Fable as designer or reviewer says so in the sentence: \"…holding it to Cordis perfection\".","created":"2026-08-19T20:04:37-04:00","changed":"2026-09-14T21:47:28-04:00","custom":{"command":"claude --model fable --dangerously-skip-permissions","role":"reviewer"}}
{"id":"agent-opencode","parent":"agents-root","ord":"a5","title":"Opencode","desc":"Kimi author and reviewer. Messages sent while it works wait until later and do not interrupt it. If it fails or emits malformed tool-call text, type continue in its prompt. Never kill or restart Opencode to recover from those failures.","changed":"2026-09-12T08:07:12-04:00","custom":{"command":"opencode","role":"author+reviewer"}}
{"id":"agent-codex","parent":"agents-root","ord":"a9","title":"Codex with GPT-6 Astra","desc":"GPT-6 Astra at medium reasoning effort. Runs this orchestrator and can also author or review when selected. Launch with --yolo as requested by the human.\n\nSending to a busy Codex: never queue with Tab. Type the sentence and press Enter; mid-turn Codex submits it as a steer after its next tool call, which is what the human wants. Escape interrupts the turn and is not for delivery. Pasted text shows as a placeholder that a first Enter expands and a second Enter submits; a plain typed line submits on one Enter.","created":"2026-09-08T10:05:43-04:00","changed":"2026-09-12T16:41:04-04:00","custom":{"command":"codex --yolo --model gpt-6-astra -c 'model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"'","role":"author+reviewer"}}
{"id":"workflow","parent":"supervision","ord":"aJ","title":"Workflow","desc":"This is the workflow template. When a workflow starts, copy this subtree into orchestrator/lanes.olai for that task, with new node IDs and wait dependencies pointing to the copied steps. Track progress and time on the copy; keep this template ready for the next task. Mark each copied step doing when it starts and done when it finishes. Keep review and CI steps open through their loops.","created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T10:30:59-04:00"}
{"id":"pes7ok2u","parent":"workflow","ord":"a0","title":"1. Human chooses the agents","todo":true,"desc":"The human picks one implementor and one or two reviewers.","created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T10:30:59-04:00"}
{"id":"1iitxclm","parent":"workflow","ord":"a1","title":"2. Start all agents","todo":true,"desc":"The orchestrator starts all two or three agents in Kolu terminals. Whenever a Kolu terminal is launched, immediately add its ID to the lane node's kolu-terminal property. Keep all terminal IDs there when a lane has multiple agents.","after":["pes7ok2u"],"created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T10:34:23-04:00"}
{"id":"lrz5werw","parent":"workflow","ord":"a2","title":"3. Implement and open a draft PR","todo":true,"desc":"The brief to the implementor is exactly this line, nothing before or after it:\n\nImplement and open a PR: <path>\n\n<path> is the plan file in the worktree. Everything else the implementor needs is in the plan and the repository's own instructions. Record the PR URL in the lane node's pr-url property. Keep this step doing until the draft PR exists and its URL is recorded.","after":["1iitxclm"],"created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-12T08:06:23-04:00"}
{"id":"5btc98w7","parent":"workflow","ord":"a3","title":"4. Review until consensus","todo":true,"desc":"Send the completed PR to the chosen reviewer or reviewers. Each reviewer posts findings in a PR comment. Send the comments back to the implementor, then send the updated PR back to the reviewers. Repeat until the implementor and all chosen reviewers agree that the findings are resolved.","after":["lrz5werw"],"created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T10:30:59-04:00"}
{"id":"hsffve7a","parent":"workflow","ord":"a4","title":"5. Refactor the PR","todo":true,"desc":"The implementor refactors the PR using https://kolu.dev/blog/hickey-lowy/: separate concepts tangled together (Hickey), and put boundaries around decisions that change independently (Löwy). Run relevant checks and hand the PR back to the orchestrator.","after":["5btc98w7"],"created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T10:30:59-04:00"}
{"id":"x7urraby","parent":"workflow","ord":"a5","title":"7. Run Odu CI until it passes","todo":true,"desc":"Run Odu CI through the Olai Odu plugin/MCP from a clean detached worktree at the exact head. After each accepted run_start, and after a run_retry that relaunches, replace the lane row's odu-run property with the run id odu answered with (run_start's runId, run_retry's effectiveRun), spelled exactly as the receipt spells it. The odu plugin boards runs by that id and rings this conversation for them; a run nobody boards is not watched, whatever checkout it ran in, and there is no path-based fallback. Rely on the plugin's chat events for failures and completion. Repeat until CI passes on the latest commit.\n\nRead every failure's log before acting. A flake (a timing or host failure outside the change, or a recipe that passed on another run of the same head) is retried with run_retry on that node, not sent to the implementor. Only a reproducible failure, or one in what the change touched, goes to the implementor, who fixes it and hands the updated PR back for another run. This holds for every repository a lane runs CI in, kolu included.","after":["workflow-merge-master"],"created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-13T09:36:24-04:00"}
{"id":"2rq29dyr","parent":"workflow","ord":"a6","title":"9. Merge the PR","todo":true,"desc":"By default, report readiness and wait for the human to merge. If the human explicitly chooses auto-merge, merge only after CI passes on the latest PR commit and the required Saatchi evidence is published and verified.","after":["workflow-saatchi"],"created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T12:56:58-04:00"}
{"id":"i67b5ppj","parent":"workflow","ord":"a7","title":"10. Close terminals and record time","todo":true,"desc":"After confirming the human's merge, close all terminals for the task. Record time spent on each step and the total on the work item, using the times recorded during the work.","after":["2rq29dyr"],"created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T12:56:58-04:00"}
{"id":"workflow-saatchi","parent":"workflow","ord":"a5V","title":"8. Verify with Saatchi before auto-merge","todo":true,"desc":"Required when the human chooses auto-merge. The implementor uses https://github.com/juspay/saatchi to capture the actual app demonstrating the requested result on the latest PR commit and publishes the evidence on the PR. The reviewer inspects the evidence and records a verification verdict with the evidence link and reviewed commit. Keep auto-merge blocked until the reviewer verifies it. If verification requires code changes, send them to the implementor and repeat CI, evidence capture and reviewer verification on the updated commit. When the human will merge manually, mark this conditional step skipped. If an existing lane switches to auto-merge, add this step before its merge step. If no reviewer was selected, obtain the human's reviewer choice before this verification.","after":["x7urraby"],"created":"2026-09-08T12:50:36-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T12:56:58-04:00"}
{"id":"workflow-merge-master","parent":"workflow","ord":"a4V","title":"6. Merge latest master","todo":true,"desc":"Before handing the PR to the orchestrator for CI, the implementor fetches and merges the latest origin/master into the PR branch, resolves any conflicts, runs relevant checks and pushes. If already up to date, record that verification. Hand back the resulting PR commit for CI. Repeat this check before each later CI handoff.","after":["hsffve7a"],"created":"2026-09-08T12:56:52-04:00"}
{"id":"dispatch-check","parent":"supervision","ord":"aK","title":"Confirm a handoff was submitted","desc":"After sending terminal text, wait for output to settle, press Enter, and inspect the terminal once to confirm the prompt was submitted or the agent is working. A successful sendInput alone is not proof. If still unsubmitted, press Enter again and verify. Do not report work underway until confirmed.\n\nCodex: a long paste shows as \"[Pasted Content N chars]\" placeholders. When idle, the first Enter expands them and a further Enter submits; an idle Codex may also auto-submit a paste. When mid-turn, press Enter anyway: Codex submits the line as a steer after its next tool call. Never press Tab to queue; the human wants every message sent at once. Escape interrupts a running turn and is not for delivery. Claude Code: a paste shows \"[Pasted text #N]\" and one Enter submits; a suggested line in its composer is Claude's own, never a human message, and typing replaces it.\n\nEvery terminal an agent is working in must be claimed by the kolu-terminal property of a doing row on the lane, or the kolu watcher never rings when it finishes. When the working terminal changes between steps, move the property to the row that is doing.","created":"2026-09-08T15:28:29-04:00","changed":"2026-09-12T17:05:40-04:00"}
{"id":"ci-required-contexts","parent":"supervision","ord":"aL","title":"Check required CI before calling a PR green","desc":"Read target-branch rules with GitHub rules/branches endpoint and compare EVERY required context with successful checks/statuses on the exact current PR head. gh pr checks and statusCheckRollup omit expected contexts that never reported. Run every required platform; never call Linux-only Odu CI full when macOS is required. Odu master requires Linux and macOS contexts; Olai master currently requires Linux and CodeQL. Re-read rules per readiness check; do not assume forever.","created":"2026-09-08T18:32:42-04:00","changed":"2026-09-13T10:04:52-04:00"}
{"id":"odu-incomplete-run","parent":"supervision","ord":"aM","title":"Read the coordinator log when Odu ends without deciding","desc":"An Odu event saying CI \"ended without deciding\" with nothing red is usually not a code failure. Read ~/.local/state/odu/runs/<runId>/coordinator.log first: a venue that stops answering keep-alive pings makes the coordinator lose its lease and exit, stopping every running node. The fix is a fresh run_start with a new requestId on the same head, not a message to the implementor. Do not pass linger: odu refuses it for sharded recipes, and a refused requestId stays consumed. If the macOS venue keeps dropping, a Linux-only run is acceptable for olai, whose master requires Linux and CodeQL contexts only; odu's master requires macOS too. An implementor's own run that ends without deciding is usually its own cancel before a rerun.","created":"2026-09-09T13:05:04-04:00","changed":"2026-09-12T08:07:03-04:00"}
{"id":"ci-host-health","parent":"supervision","ord":"aN","title":"Check CI host disk and load when unrelated tests time out","desc":"When an Odu run goes red on timeouts in tests the PR never touched, and different tests fail on each rerun, SSH the Linux venues before blaming the code: `df -h /`, `/proc/loadavg`, `/proc/pressure/io`, and `ls /tmp/odu/olai | wc -l` on each host in ~/.config/odu/hosts.json. Do not clean or reboot CI hosts; report them to the human.\n\nWorkarounds. For orchestrator full CI: run_start with hostsFile ~/.config/odu/hosts-lane-graph.json (the healthy-hosts list; trim it as hosts sicken) and supersede a doomed run. For an implementor's own `just *-fast-remote` runs: odu reads `$ODU_HOSTS` first, so tell the agent to export ODU_HOSTS=/home/srid/.config/odu/hosts-lane-graph.json. The default ~/.config/odu/hosts.json is a read-only home-manager symlink; only the human can change it. Two lanes running remote legs at once on a shrunken fleet makes timeouts worse; serialize them if it persists.","created":"2026-09-09T13:25:15-04:00","changed":"2026-09-12T08:07:07-04:00"}
{"id":"one-pr-per-lane","parent":"supervision","ord":"aO","title":"One PR per lane; never split into stacked PRs","desc":"A lane delivers ONE PR. A design's numbered plan is an ordering of commits inside that PR, never a series of PRs. A large diff still goes in one PR with one green commit per plan step; the reviewer reviews the whole PR once. Do not let an implementor stop at a split point, do not open a second PR for the same lane without the human asking, and ignore any designer or reviewer advice that implies multi-PR sequencing. \"Severable\" in a design means a later lane, not a parallel PR in this one.","created":"2026-09-09T19:02:51-04:00","changed":"2026-09-12T08:07:09-04:00"}
{"id":"implementor-never-merges","parent":"supervision","ord":"aP","title":"Implementors never merge; the guard lives in the plan and the repo, not in the brief","desc":"Implementors never merge. The guard is not in the brief: the designer writes \"one PR, opened as a draft, never merged by the implementor\" into every plan, and the repository's instructions say the same. Before handing a plan over, check it for \"land\", \"landed\" or \"merge\"; if those words could mean merging, send the plan back to the designer. A plan that sequences several PRs is rewritten to one PR first. Codex TUI: Escape interrupts a running turn; a Tab-queued message lands only after the turn; an idle Codex may auto-submit a paste. Merged PR heads stay reachable at refs/pull/N/head after a force-reset.","created":"2026-09-10T20:04:30-04:00","changed":"2026-09-12T08:06:26-04:00"}
{"id":"no-history-in-memory","parent":"supervision","ord":"aQ","title":"Memory holds rules, never history","desc":"A node in this subtree states a rule or a fact the next conversation needs, in the fewest words that make it actionable. It never narrates how the rule was learned: no incident stories, no dates, no commit shas, no PR numbers, no \"the human said\". When a rule changes, rewrite the node; do not append. Lane records in orchestrator/lanes.olai carry the timeline; this file carries none.","created":"2026-09-12T08:07:16-04:00"}
{"id":"one-sentence-briefs","parent":"supervision","ord":"aR","title":"Every prompt to an agent is one sentence; never write a file to point at","desc":"A prompt to any agent (designer, implementor, reviewer) is a single sentence. It may name one file path, and only a file another agent created (a plan, a design, a review). The orchestrator never writes a file of its own to link from a prompt, and never expands a prompt into rules, legs, hosts, or caveats; those belong in the plan or the repository's instructions. Conflicts and rulings pass the same way: one sentence naming the file the other agent amended.","created":"2026-09-12T08:07:56-04:00"}
{"id":"agent-xai-grok","parent":"agents-root","ord":"aA","title":"Grok","desc":"xAI's grok CLI, launched with --always-approve so it never stops for tool approval. It is not on the orchestrator's own PATH; the rc-hooked terminal shell has it, and the binary lives under ~/.grok/downloads/ as grok-<version>-linux-x86_64 if a launch ever needs the absolute path. Resume a past session with grok --continue in the same directory. Its idle repaint is about a second, so never judge it finished by byte silence alone; read the screen.","created":"2026-09-12T18:26:05-04:00","custom":{"command":"grok --always-approve","role":"author+reviewer"}}
{"id":"screenshots-stay-local","parent":"supervision","ord":"aS","title":"The human's screenshots never leave this system","desc":"A screenshot the human attaches in chat may show their private vault. It is read locally and its path may be named to an agent on this machine, but it is never posted, uploaded or quoted into GitHub, a PR, an issue, or any external service, by the orchestrator or by an agent it briefs. When an agent must describe the repro publicly, it describes the behaviour in its own words with test fixtures, never the human's content. Every brief that names a screenshot path says so.","created":"2026-09-12T19:03:59-04:00"}
{"id":"odu-retry-e2e-unsharded","parent":"supervision","ord":"aT","title":"Retry a red Odu e2e with a fresh run_start, not run_retry","desc":"run_retry on a settled run relaunches the e2e recipe UNSHARDED (one shard slot), so the whole browser suite runs serially on one host and takes three to four times as long as the sharded original. A shard selector (e2e[2-of-6]) is refused; the selector is the recipe (e2e@x86_64-linux); and a retry is refused while the parent run is still live. To rerun a flaky e2e on the same head, prefer a fresh run_start in the same clean checkout with a new requestId: it shards again and reuses the identical-head results of the recipes that already passed. Either way, move the lane row's odu-run to the run id the receipt answers.","created":"2026-09-13T12:23:53-04:00"}
{"id":"terminal-claims-one-id","parent":"supervision","ord":"aU","title":"A terminal claim is one id; a step that waits on the human is todo","desc":"kolu-terminal is a typed property and holds exactly one terminal id. Keep the lane row's kolu-terminal on the implementor; name the others under implementor-terminal, reviewer-terminal and designer-terminal. The doing step's kolu-terminal names whichever terminal is working right now, and moves with each handoff.\n\nA step that waits only on the human (a merge, an approval) is marked todo with its readiness written in the note, not left doing: a doing step whose terminal is idle makes the kolu watcher ring this conversation every half hour demanding a report. It goes doing again when the orchestrator has work to do on it. Odu CI for the odu repository takes platforms x86_64-linux and aarch64-darwin on run_start; a full run at both is about five minutes.","created":"2026-09-14T14:03:37-04:00"}
{"id":"prototype-means-html","parent":"supervision","ord":"aV","title":"\"Prototype\" from the human means a static .html mockup, never code in the app","desc":"When the human asks an agent to prototype a UI, the brief says a standalone .html file (a mockup that opens in a browser, no app code touched) for the human to approve before anyone implements. A brief that only says \"prototype\" sends Fable into a full implementation. Spell out the artifact in the sentence: \"as one standalone .html mockup\".","created":"2026-09-14T19:00:54-04:00"}
{"id":"agent-omp","parent":"agents-root","ord":"aB","title":"omp (Oh My Pi) on kimi-k3","desc":"The omp CLI (a pi fork) on the litellm/kimi-k3 model, chosen with --model=litellm/kimi-k3 (fuzzy match; provider/id spelling is exact). It is not on the orchestrator's own PATH; the rc-hooked terminal shell has it at ~/.local/bin/omp. No yolo flag in its help: watch the first tool calls for an approval prompt and tell the human if it asks. Resume a past session in the same directory with omp -c. Check the model name in its header when it starts.","created":"2026-09-14T19:50:49-04:00","custom":{"command":"omp --model=litellm/kimi-k3","role":"author+reviewer"}}
{"id":"agent-omp-open-fast","parent":"agents-root","ord":"aC","title":"omp (Oh My Pi) on open-fast","desc":"The omp CLI on the litellm/open-fast model, chosen with --model=litellm/open-fast (provider/id spelling is exact). Same handling as the kimi-k3 entry: not on the orchestrator's own PATH (the rc-hooked terminal shell has it at ~/.local/bin/omp); no yolo flag in its help, so watch the first tool calls for an approval prompt and tell the human if it asks; resume a past session in the same directory with omp -c; check the model name in its header when it starts.","created":"2026-09-15T16:51:01-04:00","custom":{"command":"omp --model=litellm/open-fast","role":"author+reviewer"}}
{"id":"implementor-no-full-ci","parent":"supervision","ord":"aW","title":"Implementors never run full CI; the orchestrator's CI step does, once, from a clean checkout","desc":"An implementor iterates with the fast-remote legs (just typecheck-fast-remote, test-fast-remote, e2e-fast-remote) and never runs `just ci` after its commits; the repository's CLAUDE.md says so. Full CI runs once, in the lane's CI step, from a clean detached worktree at the exact head, by the orchestrator. A plan that tells the implementor to keep each commit green on `just ci` contradicts this: the guard check on every plan also looks for \"just ci\" and sends the plan back to the designer to say fast-remote instead. An implementor already running per-commit CI is told in one sentence naming CLAUDE.md.","created":"2026-09-15T21:45:37-04:00"}
{"id":"omp-half-hour-check","parent":"supervision","ord":"aX","title":"While an omp implements, check its terminal every 30 minutes on a cron","desc":"The kolu doorbell rings only when a terminal goes idle, so a long omp turn is silent. While any omp implements, a 30-minute cron in this conversation (CronCreate, off-minutes 13 and 43) reads each working omp terminal's screen, writes the progress (stage, commits, PR, spend shown in its footer) on the lane's implement step, and tells the human if one is stalled, looping, or stuck at an approval prompt. Delete the cron when no omp is implementing. The doorbell still handles idle terminals; the cron never types into a terminal. The cron is session-only and expires after seven days: a new session with an omp still implementing recreates it first thing.","created":"2026-09-15T21:48:24-04:00","changed":"2026-09-16T04:20:45-04:00","custom":{"cron":"13,43 * * * *","cron-id":"none — deleted 08:23 UTC 2026-09-16 when no omp was implementing; recreate when one is"}}
